Cell["\<\
Figure 6.1  Monthly centroid ground speed for each species along its primary \
flyway. The tall passage peaks (Arctic tern ~135 km/day; red knot ~74 km/day) \
separate the near-zero plateaux of the breeding and wintering seasons.\
\>", "Text",
 CellMargins->{{70, 70}, {2, 14}},
 FontSize->11,
 FontSlant->Italic,
 FontColor->GrayLevel[
  0.35],ExpressionUUID->"de8bfc1b-c09a-4192-9db8-d25573caee1b"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Crucial caveat \[LongDash] this is a lower bound. ",
  FontWeight->Bold,
  FontSlant->Italic],
 "Centroid speed is the speed of the population ",
 StyleBox["centre of mass",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 ", which is a ",
 StyleBox["lower bound",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " on individual ground speed: when birds in different parts of the range \
move in opposing directions, those motions cancel in the mean, so the \
centroid moves more slowly than any of them. Equally, the low-speed months \
are not necessarily true en-route \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]stopovers\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] \[LongDash] they mostly mark the breeding/wintering \
turn-around, when the whole population sits still. Read these as the tempo of \
the population statistic, not of an individual bird."
}], "Text",
 CellFrame->{{4, 0}, {0, 0}},
 CellMargins->{{60, 60}, {6, 10}},
 CellFrameColor->RGBColor[0.85, 0.6, 0.15],
 Background->RGBColor[
  1., 0.97, 0.88],ExpressionUUID->"4b147917-453f-4535-a86e-eedf58e6a18e"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["6.1  Does day length pace the journey?", "Subsection",
 FontFamily->"Roboto Condensed",
 FontColor->RGBColor[
  0.153, 0.51, 0.64],ExpressionUUID->"8d9fa547-ebb2-4d1e-b1b4-694fddaf581d"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A natural hypothesis: migrants might travel fastest when the photoperiod is \
changing fastest, since day length is the most reliable seasonal cue a bird \
has. We can test it directly against our own numbers \[LongDash] correlate \
each month's centroid speed with that month's rate of change of day length. \
",
 StyleBox["The honest answer is: not really.",
  FontSlant->Italic],
 " Across the twelve monthly steps the correlation is weak, and under a \
cyclic permutation that respects the annual cycle (the only null that does \
not pretend the twelve months are independent draws), it is ",
 StyleBox["not significant",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ". The red knot is the strongest case, at ",
 StyleBox["r = 0.71",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 ", and even there ",
 StyleBox["p = 0.08",
  FontWeight->Bold],
 " (n = 12). Suggestive at best \[LongDash] a hypothesis to take to tracking \
data, not a result to report. (With only twelve months, the cyclic \
permutation has just twelve distinct rotations, so the smallest p it can \
return is 1/12 \[TildeEqual] 0.083: this test can never reach p < 0.05, which \
is itself an honest admission of how little a 12-point annual series can \
prove.)"
}], "Text",
 CellMargins->{{50, 50}, {6, 
  6}},ExpressionUUID->"c8c9530a-94cf-48a0-b147-5f5a7f227e9b"],

